WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.802
Suppose the prices of phone cases and strawberries both jump. A factory can make more phone cases this week. A farmer cannot grow more strawberries until the next season.

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:26.410
Price elasticity of supply, or P E S, measures how much quantity supplied responds to a price change. Divide the percentage change in quantity supplied by the percentage change in price.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:41.610
Read the value. Nought is perfectly inelastic. Between nought and one, inelastic. Exactly one, unitary. Above one, elastic. Infinity, perfectly elastic.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:49.258
Elastic supply is drawn flat. When demand rises, quantity supplied rises a lot and price only a little.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:58.362
Inelastic supply is drawn steep. The same rise in demand mostly pushes up the price, and quantity rises only a little.

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:14.625
Define P E S and classify the value. Then apply the reasons. Supply is more elastic when firms have spare capacity, hold stocks, can easily get more factors of production, or have more time.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:33.122
Primary products like crops take time to grow, depend on the weather and often cannot be stored. So their supply is usually inelastic. Factories can add shifts and hold stocks, so manufactured goods are usually more elastic.

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:45.375
Steps four and five. Use the case data. In an Assess question, weigh what could change the picture, such as full capacity or a longer time period, then judge.

00:01:46.480 --> 00:02:09.823
Here is a real question from June 2024. Bangladesh's textile factories often work below capacity. About forty per cent of people work in farming, and crops are produced once a year. Assess whether supply is more price elastic for textiles than for farm products. Nine marks.

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:26.353
Textiles first. The factories work below capacity, so if the price rises they can add a shift and use spare machines. Shirts can also be stored and sold later. Supply is elastic.

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:40.395
Now farm products. Wheat and corn are grown once a year, output depends on the weather, and fruit is perishable. So output cannot rise quickly. Supply is inelastic.

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:47.727
On a diagram, wheat has a steep supply curve. Higher demand mostly raises the price, not the quantity.

00:02:48.280 --> 00:03:01.208
Now weigh it. If the factories reach full capacity, textile supply becomes less elastic. And over time, with so many farm workers, farmers can plant more for the next season.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:15.587
Judge. Textile supply is likely to be more elastic, especially in the short run. Over a longer period the gap narrows. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:25.516
A common mistake is calling a P E S of nought point seven elastic. It is below one, so supply is inelastic.

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:35.233
Another is assuming a factory can always raise output. At full capacity it needs new machines and workers, which takes time.

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:54.867
Now you try. A bakery has spare ovens and can bake extra loaves within hours. A vineyard needs three years for new vines to produce grapes. Which has more price elastic supply, and why? Pause and decide.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:10.655
The bakery. Its spare ovens let it raise output within hours, so its supply is elastic. The vineyard must wait three years for new vines, so its supply is inelastic.

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:20.061
Remember: define and classify P E S, apply the reasons to each product, then weigh and judge. Now try the practice questions.
